  "prompt": "Which methods did Socrates employ to challenge the prevailing thoughts of his time?",
  "answer": "Socrates challenged the prevailing ideas of his time mainly through critical dialogue:\n\n- **Socratic questioning:** He asked probing questions about familiar concepts—such as justice, courage, and virtue—to expose assumptions and demand clearer definitions.\n- **Elenchus, or cross-examination:** He tested people’s claims against their other beliefs, revealing contradictions that forced them to reconsider their positions.\n- **Socratic irony:** He often presented himself as someone who lacked knowledge, inviting supposedly knowledgeable people to explain themselves and reveal the limits of their understanding.\n- **Reasoning rather than appeals to authority:** He questioned whether beliefs were justified, even when they were supported by tradition, prominent citizens, or popular opinion.\n- **Ethical self-examination:** He urged people to examine their own lives and priorities, challenging the assumption that wealth, reputation, or power mattered more than virtue.\n\nHis questioning often led to **aporia**—a recognition of uncertainty—which encouraged further inquiry rather than easy acceptance of conventional answers.",
